      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "If \n$C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}]$, then $\\text{rank}(C)=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the rank of $C,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(C)=2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
