    "q-34763": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34763",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "If $D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,$ then $\\text{rank}(D)=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the rank of $D,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(D)=3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
